sec.p:
To evaluate the functional response of resident immune effector cells and MM plasma cell lysis, tumor cell lysis and T‐cell activation as well as cytokine assays were performed using BMA from MM patient donors independent of the forimtamig clinical trial. Two distinct protocols were employed in different laboratories, differing primarily in sample preparation steps while following a similar workflow for treatment and analysis (see 
Supplementary Methods
).

Cytokine release upon T‐cell activation was measured by cytometric bead array technology following the manufacturer's instructions (see 
Supplementary Methods
).

Similar to the in vitro assays, incubation of forimtamig with BMA samples from MM patient donors (independent of the clinical trial) led to tumor cell lysis, T‐cell activation, and cytokine release (Table

S3
).

Forimtamig‐induced depletion of MM cells in 9 of 12 assessed BMA samples (Figure

1
a,b
). The mean EC50 for tumor cell lysis was 52.3 pM. Forimtamig also induced dose‐dependent activation of tumor‐infiltrating T cells (Figure

1
c–e
), with EC50 values ranging from 0.4 to 45 pM for CD69+ T cells. Finally, forimtamig‐induced IL‐6 levels of 12.7 pM (< 300 pg/mL) following incubation with BMA samples from individuals with MM, up to the highest tested dose of 7 nM (Figure

1
f
).

caption.p:
(a, b) Representative forimtamig‐induced lysis of MM patient donor cells after incubation for (a) 36 and (b) 42 hours (see protocols 1 and 2 in 
Supplementary Methods
). Each curve represents one patient donor sample. (c–e) Representative forimtamig‐induced activation of autologous T cells from MM patient donors after incubation for (c) 36 and (d, e) 42 hours. Each curve represents one patient donor sample. (f) Quantitative analysis of IL‐6 release following incubation of BMA samples from MM patient donors with forimtamig. Each dot represents one patient donor. 70 pM of a T‐cell bispecific antibody isotype was evaluated in parallel as a negative reference. The theoretical detection limit was 1.6 pg/mL, and the limit of the kit was 10 mg/mL. (g) Individual EC50 values of forimtamig for tumor cell lysis in BMA samples from nine MM patient donors after incubation for 36–42 hours. Each dot represents one patient donor sample. BMA, bone marrow aspirate; CI, confidence interval; EC50, half maximal effective concentration; IL‐6, interleukin 6; MM, multiple myeloma.

sec.p:
By this means, the second patient received a Cycle 1 Day 1 dose of 18 μg and a target dose of 54 μg and showed transient decreases of free light chains and M‐protein (Figure

2
), indicating early clinical activity but not qualifying as an objective response per IMWG criteria. The fourth and fifth patients (Cohort 3), treated with a Cycle 1 Day 1 dose of 54 μg and a target dose of 162 μg, achieved a very good partial response (VGPR) and stringent complete response (sCR) with response durations of 9.1 months and 37.9 months, respectively.

Coupled with considerable inter‐MM patient donor heterogeneity, our small number of BMA samples yielded a wide range of results, as seen in the tumor cell lysis assay. Our recommendation is to ideally source at least 15 or even 30 MM patient donor samples to mitigate this effect. Based on a simulation approach, using n ≥ 15 allows specification of a lower limit of EC50 values which is expected to be undercut for at most 20% of the population with 95% statistical confidence. Using n ≥ 30 would limit this risk to 10% of the population if that level is required (see 
Supplementary Methods
).

Strengths of the ex vivo MABEL approach include a more physiological reflection of the in vivo microenvironment and the possibility of more patients in Phase 1 trials receiving a dose that is clinically active as well as safe. Developing a new approach in drug development is risky; we share our learnings (Box

1
) to support others in taking a similarly patient‐centric approach to achieve the best results for clinical trial participants.
